---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { vendors, priced, money, VERIFIED_LABEL, KLASS_LABEL } from '../data/site.js';

const emr = vendors.filter(v => v.klass === 'emr');
const booking = vendors.filter(v => v.klass === 'booking');
const withSetup = vendors.filter(v => v.setupFee);
const promo = vendors.filter(v => v.promo);
const cheapest = priced.slice(0, 6);
---
<Base
  title="MedSpaStack — independent pricing research on med spa software"
  description="Verified pricing, hidden fees and clinical-capability data for 24 med spa and aesthetics practice management platforms. Collected from vendor pages, dated, and updated weekly."
>
<h1>What med spa software actually costs</h1>

<p class="lede">
  We track published pricing, setup fees and add-on charges for {vendors.length} med spa and
  aesthetics platforms, and record which ones have real medical charting versus which are
  booking systems wearing a clinical label. Every number carries the date we read it off the
  vendor's own page.
</p>

<div class="note accent">
  <p>
    <strong>Why this site exists.</strong> Advertised starting prices in this category are close to
    meaningless. Of the {priced.length} platforms that publish a price at all, {promo.length} are
    quoting a promotional or annual-prepay rate rather than their standard one, several bill per
    location or per user rather than per business, and nearly every one charges separately for the
    features a med spa actually needs — charting, e-prescribing, forms, marketing. We publish the
    whole bill, not the headline.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>Start here</h2>
<div class="cards">
  <a class="card" href="/pricing/">
    <div class="n">The pricing database</div>
    <div class="p">All {vendors.length} platforms, every published tier, setup fees and add-on costs in one table.</div>
  </a>
  <a class="card" href="/emr-vs-booking/">
    <div class="n">EMR or booking system?</div>
    <div class="p">{emr.length} of these have real medical charting. {booking.length} do not. This is the decision most buyers get wrong first.</div>
  </a>
  <a class="card" href="/cost-calculator/">
    <div class="n">Real cost calculator</div>
    <div class="p">Enter your locations, providers and the modules you need. See the twelve-month total, not the sticker price.</div>
  </a>
  <a class="card" href="/methodology/">
    <div class="n">How we collect this</div>
    <div class="p">Where the numbers come from, what we refuse to score, and who pays us.</div>
  </a>
</div>

<h2>Lowest published starting prices</h2>
<p>
  Sorted by advertised entry price. Read the billing note before drawing conclusions — several of
  these are per user or per location, which changes the maths completely once you have a team.
</p>

<div class="scroll">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Platform</th><th>Type</th><th class="num">From</th><th>Billing basis</th><th class="num">Setup fee</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {cheapest.map(v => (
      <tr>
        <td><a href={`/software/${v.slug}/`}>{v.displayName}</a></td>
        <td><span class={`tag ${v.klass}`}>{KLASS_LABEL[v.klass]}</span></td>
        <td class="num">{money(v.startingPrice)}/mo</td>
        <td>{(v.entryNote || '—').split(';')[0]}</td>
        <td class="num">{v.setupFee ? money(v.setupFee) : '—'}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p><a href="/pricing/">See all {vendors.length} platforms →</a></p>

<h2>The three most expensive assumptions</h2>

<h3>1. That the advertised price is per business</h3>
<p>
  It usually is not. Boulevard, Mangomint, Mindbody and Square Appointments price
  <em>per location</em>. Aesthetic Record and Fresha price <em>per user</em> — Aesthetic Record's
  $15 entry tier becomes $90 a month for a six-person clinic before a single add-on, and it carries
  a $399 onboarding fee that appears nowhere near the headline. Two platforms with similar sticker
  prices can differ threefold on the invoice.
</p>

<h3>2. That clinical charting is included</h3>
<p>
  Fresha, Vagaro, Square Appointments, WellnessLiving, Mindbody and Booker have no medical
  charting at all. They are excellent booking systems. If you inject, laser or prescribe, you will
  be buying a second system — a cost that rarely appears in any comparison table.
  <a href="/emr-vs-booking/">We sorted every platform on this axis.</a>
</p>

<h3>3. That the annual price is the price</h3>
<p>
  Most published rates in this category assume annual prepayment, and several are promotional.
  Boulevard's listed $140 tier is a summer promotion against a $176 standard rate, on annual
  billing, per location. We record the standard rate alongside the promotional one.
</p>

<hr />
<p class="stamp">
  Pricing last verified {VERIFIED_LABEL} against each vendor's own published pricing page.
  Vendors change prices without notice; we recheck weekly and stamp every figure.
  Spotted something stale or wrong? <a href="/about/">Tell us</a> — corrections get published.
</p>
</Base>
